cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark or its results, please cite this work."
title: "The Concrete–Arbitrary Gap: Kinship Reasoning in LLMs Is Not Indifferent to Presentation"
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: Pashby
    given-names: Thomas
    affiliation: Middlebury College
    email: tpashby@middlebury.edu
year: 2026
abstract: >-
  We test whether large language models solve formally matched kinship
  problems equally well when relations are expressed in familiar vocabulary
  or by explicitly defined nonce predicates. Across four models and 500 paired
  graphs, concrete accuracy reliably exceeds arbitrary accuracy. Reasoning
  budgets and prompt-language interventions can substantially reduce the
  difference, showing that it is modifiable rather than a fixed incapacity.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - relational reasoning
  - kinship inference
  - semantic content
  - reasoning budgets
  - representation sensitivity
repository-code: https://github.com/tompashby/concrete-arbitrary-gap
url: https://github.com/tompashby/concrete-arbitrary-gap
